    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with a high-ceiling lineup led by **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.**, whose 80.22 efficiency score is the class of the roster. Facing Liberatore (xERA 4.28), **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** benefits from a favorable park factor and a premium spot in the two-hole, making him our safest anchor for R&B/HR production. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** (74.56) and **Cole Carrigg** (71.23) follow closely, both capitalizing on sub-5.00 xERA matchups and advantageous park factors. Specifically, **Cole Carrigg** thrives today due to a rare intersection of park-adjusted power and the +3% bonus assigned to his switch-hitting profile, positioning him as a high-upside play against Lugo.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the optimizer occurred at the final OF slot, where **Heliot Ramos** (58.34) just missed the cut, losing out to the efficiency of **Jackson Merrill** (62.77) and **Kyle Tucker** (62.57). While **Heliot Ramos** remains a viable start, his matchup against Michael King involves a restrictive hold-pitcher penalty that depressed his score compared to **Kyle Tucker**, whose elite base projection carries him into the lineup despite the challenging seventh-order slot. Meanwhile, we are closely monitoring the 41% rain risk surrounding **Gunnar Henderson**. Despite the weather uncertainty and the presence of an elite catcher affecting his stolen base potential, his high park factor and favorable position in the heart of the order keep him locked in as a start. \n\nFinally, managers should keep a close eye on our catchers, **Liam Hicks** and **Drake Baldwin**, both of whom are active today. **Liam Hicks** (59.93) carries a strong projection bolstered by a favorable matchup against Stock (xERA 9.14), whereas **Drake Baldwin** (51.79) is a marginal play who serves primarily to fill our slots against a tougher arm in Cavalli. We are benching **Luis Robert Jr.** (50.58) and **Sam Antonacci** (47.11); while both possess the speed we crave, they simply couldn't outpace the efficiency gains offered by the middle-infield stack of **Jacob Wilson** (68.50) and **Nasim Nunez** (64.28) in today's specific pitching environment.",
